  See LICENSE for the full text.

The Runtime Library Exception is what permits redistribution of a compiled
Swift runtime without attaching the Apache 2.0 attribution requirements to
programs that link against it.

WHAT IS AND ISN'T REDISTRIBUTED HERE
  * The shipped libswiftCore.dylib is compiled from unmodified Swift
    sources (the release pinned in pins.env) PLUS the local patches in ./patches/runtime.
  * NO Apple prebuilt binaries are redistributed. The swift.org toolchain and
    the macOS SDK are used only as *build inputs* on the builder's machine;
    none of their bytes are included in this artifact.

Local modifications (full diffs in ./patches/runtime) — 0001-0007 all confined to making
Swift's runtime work on 10.9's objc4-532, all gated to the 10.9/10.10 code path; 0008
keeps a runtime built on OS X 10.9 identical to the one CI builds:
  0001  unsized operator delete — emit operator delete(void*) instead of the
        C++14 sized operator delete(void*, size_t), absent from 10.9's libc++.
  0002  os_system_version 10.9 fallback — guard the macOS-10.10 symbol
        os_system_version_get_current_version; fall back to the SystemVersion
        plist so `if #available` works on 10.9.
  0003  objc_readClassPair guard + minimal in-place realization — 10.9 lacks the
        10.11 SPI; realize runtime-instantiated generic classes in objc4-532's
        class_rw_t layout instead.
  0004  realization-aware getROData — follow rw->ro when RW_REALIZED, because
        objc4-532 realizes classes eagerly at image load.
  0005  objc-super instance-size fix — size subclasses from
        class_getInstanceSize(super) on 10.9, where objc4-532 drops the Swift
        is-swift bit and the default path under-sizes them.
  0007  is-swift mask legacy bit — tag and check the legacy is-swift bit
        (value 1) on sub-10.14.4 targets, matching what the compiler emits
        for them; the root cause 0005 works around.
  0008  drop @DebugDescription — never compile ObjectIdentifier's LLDB type
        summary, whose macro needs swift-syntax, which the 10.9-hosted toolchain
        lacks; and keep a String fast path that the same missing macro support
        would compile out. Lost: LLDB's ObjectIdentifier summary.

Build configuration (threading package = pthreads, deployment target = 10.9,
availability flag) is recorded in MANIFEST and build.sh.

The swift.org toolchain installer attached to each new upstream's first release
(upstream-swift-*-RELEASE-osx.pkg) is a byte-for-byte mirror of the official
installer, kept so builds remain reproducible if swift.org prunes older
releases. It bundles components under several licenses (the Swift runtime
libraries: Apache-2.0 WITH Runtime Library Exception; LLVM/clang/lldb:
Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception; others under their own); it is redistributed
unmodified, its SHA256 matching upstream's. It is host build tooling and is not
part of the runtime .pkg. Upstream: https://download.swift.org/

The toolchain package (swift-toolchain-*.pkg) also contains the Swift compiler
(swift-frontend; Apache-2.0 WITH Runtime Library Exception, like this repo), and
LLVM's clang and lld (clang, ld64.lld; Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception), built from
swiftlang/llvm-project with the patches in ./patches/llvm, and compiler-rt's
builtins library (lib/clang/<version>/lib/darwin/libclang_rt.osx.a; the same
license), built from the same source; LLVM's license ships beside them as
share/doc/LICENSE-LLVM.txt. Of those patches, 0004 changes only compiler-rt's
build: it passes extra compiler flags to the Darwin builtins (whose build
otherwise drops them), so that the archive, and the runtime that links part of
it, carry no path from the machine that built them.
